          "text": [
            "Llama.generate: prefix-match hit\n"
          ]
        },
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "'\\nAccording to the Merck Manual, sepsis is a life-threatening condition caused by a dysregulated response to infection. The following are general guidelines for managing sepsis in a critical care unit:\\n1. Early recognition and prompt initiation of treatment: Recognize sepsis early based on clinical suspicion and laboratory findings such as fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, altered mental status, lactic acidosis, and leukocytosis or leukopenia. Initiate antibiotic therapy as soon as possible based on the suspected infection site and local microbiology data.\\n2. Fluid resuscitation: Administer intravenous fluids to maintain adequate tissue perfusion and organ function. The goal is to achieve a mean arterial pressure (MAP) of 65 mm Hg or higher and a central venous pressure (CVP) of 8-12 mm Hg in adults. Use crystalloids initially, followed by colloids if further fluid resuscitation is required.\\n3. Vasopressor support: If the patient does not respond to fluid resuscitation, administer vasopressors to maintain adequate blood pressure and organ perfusion. Commonly used vasopressors include norepinephrine, phenylephrine, and dopamine.\\n4. Corticosteroids: Consider using corticosteroids in patients with sepsis who have persistent hypotension despite adequate fluid resuscitation and vasopressor support. Dexamethasone is a commonly used steroid.\\n5. Inotropic agents: Use inotropic agents such as dobutamine or milrinone to improve cardiac output in patients with sepsis who have low cardiac output despite adequate fluid resuscitation and vasopressor support.\\n6. Mechanical ventilation: Provide mechanical ventilation to maintain adequate oxygenation and ventilation in patients with sepsis who have respiratory failure.\\n7. Renal replacement therapy: Consider using renal replacement therapy (RRT) in patients with sepsis who have acute kidney injury or severe metabolic disturbances that are not responding to conventional treatments.\\n8. Source control: Identify and address the source of infection, such as removing an infected catheter or draining an abscess'"
            ],
            "application/vnd.google.colaboratory.intrinsic+json": {
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 14
        }
