        "### Query 2: What are the common symptoms for appendicitis, and can it be cured via medicine? If not, what surgical procedure should be followed to treat it?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "WPPpDM6cOeff",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 140
        },
        "outputId": "a06f5e5d-b348-440a-ae5e-84c43fc6e3e2"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stderr",
          "text": [
            "Llama.generate: prefix-match hit\n"
          ]
        },
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "'\\nAccording to Merck Manuals, appendicitis is an inflammatory condition of the vermiform appendix, a small, finger-like structure that extends from the cecum in the right lower abdomen. The symptoms of appendicitis can vary but typically include:\\n1. Sudden onset of pain, usually starting around the navel and then shifting to the right lower abdomen.\\n2. Loss of appetite and feeling sick to your stomach (nausea).\\n3. Fever, which may be low-grade at first but can rise as high as 101°F (38.3°C) or more.\\n4. Vomiting, which may help relieve abdominal pain temporarily.\\n5. Constipation or diarrhea.\\n6. Abdominal swelling and rigidity.\\n7. Inability to pass gas or have a bowel movement.\\n8. Pain on walking or moving around.\\n9. Feeling restless or unable to get comfortable.\\nAppendicitis cannot be cured with medicine alone. If left untreated, the appendix may rupture, leading to peritonitis, a serious inflammation of the abdominal cavity that can be life-threatening. Therefore, surgical removal of the appendix (appendectomy) is the standard treatment for appendicitis. The procedure can be performed as an open surgery or laparoscopically, depending on the severity and location of the inflammation. During the procedure, the surgeon makes a small incision in the abdomen, removes the appendix, and closes the incision with sutures or staples. After the surgery, patients are usually advised to rest and avoid strenuous activities for several days to allow for proper healing. Complications from appendectomy are rare but can include infection, bleeding, or damage to nearby organs.'"
            ],
            "application/vnd.google.colaboratory.intrinsic+json": {
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 24
