          ]
        },
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "'\\nAccording to Merck Manuals, the following are potential causes for sudden patchy hair loss:\\n\\n1. Alopecia Areata: An autoimmune disorder that results in round patches of complete hair loss on the scalp or other areas of the body. The cause is unknown but may be related to stress, genetics, or other factors.\\n2. Trauma or injury: Physical damage to the hair follicles can result in sudden patchy hair loss. This can include burns, cuts, or pulling out hair too forcefully.\\n3. Medications: Certain medications such as chemotherapy drugs, anticoagulants, and retinoids can cause hair loss as a side effect.\\n4. Nutritional deficiencies: Lack of essential nutrients like iron, zinc, or vitamin B12 can lead to hair loss.\\n5. Hormonal imbalances: Thyroid disorders, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), and other hormonal imbalances can cause sudden patchy hair loss.\\n6. Infections: Fungal infections like ringworm or bacterial infections can lead to hair loss.\\n7. Stress: Emotional or physical stress can trigger sudden patchy hair loss.\\n\\nEffective treatments for addressing sudden patchy hair loss include:\\n\\n1. Corticosteroids: Topical or injected corticosteroids can help stimulate hair growth in alopecia areata.\\n2. Minoxidil: A topical medication that can help promote hair regrowth in some cases of androgenetic alopecia and alopecia areata.\\n3. Hair transplantation: In severe cases, hair transplantation may be an option to restore lost hair.\\n4. Nutritional supplements: Supplementing the diet with essential nutrients like iron, zinc, or vitamin B12 can help promote hair growth.\\n5. Hormonal therapy: Treating underlying hormonal imbalances with medication can help address hair loss caused by hormonal issues.\\n6. Antifungal medications: Treating fungal infections with antifungal medications can help restore lost hair.\\n7. Reducing stress: Managing stress through relaxation techniques, exercise, or counseling can help reduce the risk'"
