          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "'\\nAccording to Merck Manuals, treatment for a person with a brain injury depends on the severity and location of the injury. For mild traumatic brain injuries, also known as concussions, rest, hydration, and avoiding activities that worsen symptoms are recommended. For moderate to severe brain injuries, emergency treatment may be necessary to address life-threatening conditions such as airway obstruction, bleeding, or swelling in the brain.\\nSubsequent treatments may include:\\n1. Medications to manage symptoms such as pain, seizures, or increased intracranial pressure.\\n2. Rehabilitation therapies including physical therapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy, and cognitive rehabilitation to help restore function and improve quality of life.\\n3. Surgery may be necessary for certain types of brain injuries, such as removing hematomas or repairing skull fractures.\\n4. Assistive devices and technology, such as wheelchairs, braces, or communication aids, may be needed to help the person function optimally.\\n5. Psychological support and counseling may also be necessary to help the person cope with the emotional and social challenges of brain injury.\\nIt is important for medical professionals to closely monitor the progress of a person with a brain injury and adjust treatments as needed based on their individual response to therapy.'"
