              "'\\nI. Question: What are the common side effects of taking Metformin?\\n\\nA. According to Merck Manuals, the most common side effects of Metformin include:\\n1. Gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain or discomfort. These symptoms usually occur at the beginning of treatment and may be reduced by taking the medication with meals or by using a slow-release formulation.\\n2. Lactic acidosis is a rare but serious side effect that can occur when Metformin accumulates in the body due to kidney dysfunction. Symptoms include rapid, deep breathing; muscle cramps; and weakness or feeling cold, clammy, and confused. If you experience these symptoms, seek medical help immediately.\\n3. Other less common side effects include headache, dizziness, and a metallic taste in the mouth.\\n4. Hypoglycemia (low blood sugar) can occur if Metformin is taken with certain other medications or if meals are skipped. Symptoms of hypoglycemia include sweating, shaking, confusion, and rapid heartbeat. If you experience these symptoms, eat a quick source of sugar such as glucose tablets or fruit juice and contact your healthcare provider.\\n5. Metformin may also cause a decrease in vitamin B12 absorption, which can lead to anemia and neurological symptoms. Your healthcare provider may recommend taking a vitamin B12 supplement while taking Metformin.'"
