          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "\"Answer:\\nThe management of sepsis in a critical care unit involves several steps. First, supportive care is provided which includes adequate nutrition, prevention of infection, stress ulcers and gastritis, and pulmonary embolism (Chapter 222, Introduction). Patient monitoring and testing are crucial, with some being manual and intermittent, such as measurement of vital signs and quantification of fluid intake and output, while others are ongoing and continuous using complex devices that require special training to operate (Chapter 222, Patient Monitoring and Testing).\\n\\nBlood tests are an essential part of sepsis management. ICU patients typically have routine daily blood tests which include electrolytes and a CBC. Patients with arrhythmias should also have Mg, phosphate, and Ca levels measured. Patients receiving TPN need weekly liver enzymes and coagulation profiles (Chapter 222, Blood Tests). Point-of-care testing can be used to do certain blood tests at the patient's bedside or unit, particularly in ICUs, emergency departments, and operating rooms (Chapter 222, Blood Tests).\\n\\nEmerging therapies for severe sepsis include cooling for hyperthermia and early treatment of renal failure using continuous venovenous hemofiltration. However, trials of monoclonal antibodies to the lipid A fraction of endotoxin, antileukotrienes, and antibodies to tumor necrosis factor have been unsuccessful (Chapter 227).\\n\\nTherefore, the protocol for managing sepsis in a critical care unit involves providing supportive care, performing regular patient monitoring and testing, administering appropriate blood tests, and considering emerging therapies as needed.\""
