              "'I. For nerve regeneration:\\nA. Injections of autologous, incubated macrophages\\nB. Epidural administration of BA-210\\nC. Oral administration of HP-184\\nD. Stem cell research\\nII. Emotional care is fundamental to the success of all other components of rehabilitation and must be accompanied by efforts to educate the patient and encourage active involvement of family and friends.\\nIII. Exercises to increase endurance for patients with COPD can benefit from strategies to simplify activities and conserve energy. Activities and exercises that encourage use of upper and lower extremities are used to increase muscle aerobic capacity, which decreases overall oxygen requirement and eases breathing. Supervising patients while they engage in activity helps motivate them and makes them feel more secure.\\nIV. Early intervention by rehabilitation specialists is indispensable for maximal functional recovery from a head injury or spinal cord injury. Such intervention includes prevention of secondary disabilities, prevention of pneumonia, and family education. Patients with severe cognitive dysfunction require extensive cognitive therapy which is often begun immediately after injury and continued for months or years.\\nV. For spinal cord injuries:\\nA. Complete transsection causes flaccid paralysis\\nB. Partial transsection causes spastic paralysis of muscles innervated by the affected segment\\nC. Functional capacity depends on the level of injury and development of complications\\nD. Affected area must be immobilized surgically or nonsurgically as soon as possible and throughout the acute phase\\nE. Daily routine care during the acute phase includes measures to prevent contractures, pressure ulcers, and pneumonia; all measures needed to prevent other complications.'"
